tune Global 500", "year": 2025, "rank": 219, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2025, "rank": 67, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2025, "rank": 30, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2025, "rank": 38, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2024, "rank": 236, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500--health-care", "title": "Fortune Sector Leaders: Health Care", "year": 2024, "rank": 13, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2024, "rank": 69, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2024, "rank": 45, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2024, "rank": 20, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2023, "rank": 8, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2023, "rank": 102, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2023, "rank": 38, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2023, "rank": 38, "itemDescription": "The pharmaceutical giant rode its groundbreaking mRNA COVID-19 vaccine and antiviral pill Paxlovid to record revenue in 2022; the $100.3 billion it raked in (up from $81.3 billion in 2021) catapulted the company 40 spots on this year\u2019s Fortune 500 list.<br />\r\n<ul><br />\r\n \t<li>Pfizer is planning to sell its COVID-19 vaccine commercially in 2023, rather than contracting with the U.S. government, with a target price between $110 and $130 per dose.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>Still, it expects revenue to drop as the demand for COVID vaccines eases in 2023. Pfizer has a slate of other new drugs in its pipeline, such as the Respiratory Syncytial Virus (RSV) vaccine and medication for migraines.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>The company is expected to lose patent protections for some key drugs, including Ibrance, used for breast cancer treatment, between 2025 and 2030.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>It has acquired other biotech or smaller pharma companies to bolster its pipeline in the future. Key 2022 deals include the $5.4 billion purchase of Global Blood Therapeutics and the $11.6 billion acquisition of Biohaven.</li><br />\r\n</ul>"}, {"slug": "100-fastest-growing-companies", "title": "100 Fastest-Growing Companies", "year": 2023, "rank": 85, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2022, "rank": 4, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 137, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 43, "itemDescription": "<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Pfizer\u2019s role in responding to COVID-19 has returned it to the forefront of the pharmaceutical industry\u2014and helped it climb 34 spots on the Fortune 500 this year. As of April 2022, approximately 3.3 billion Pfizer-BioNTech COVID-19 vaccines had been shipped to 179 countries, and some 338 million doses had been administered in the U.S. alone. From 2020 to 2021 its revenue nearly doubled, to $81.3 billion, with the vaccine accounting for the bulk of that gain. Pfizer\u2019s oral antiviral pill, Paxlovid, which has been shown to reduce the risk of hospitalization or death from COVID by about 90%, is likely to join the vaccine in boosting its bottom line: As of April 2022, the Biden administration has committed to purchasing 20 million treatment courses of Paxlovid.</span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2021, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 281, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;How do you balance being one of the world\u2019s saviors during a pandemic without being perceived as gloating or, more important, gouging a global population in need? That\u2019s the tricky needle Pfizer has had to thread, given the company\u2019s status as one of the most effective coronavirus vaccine manufacturers in the world. Pfizer recorded 2020 revenues of about $42 billion\u2014but that largely excludes the post-winter period, when Pfizer\u2019s COVID vaccine was granted emergency use authorization by the FDA. The company expects to bring in $26 billion in COVID vaccine sales alone this year, about 60% of its total 2020 revenues, thanks to its sprawling relationships with governments and pandemic-focused organizations across the globe. An added benefit? Via its partnership with Germany\u2019s BioNTech, the maker of the \u201clittle blue pill\u201d is now synonymous with mRNA vaccine technology that could feed its pipeline in infectious disease treatments and other therapeutics for years to come.&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:12673,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\">How do you balance being one of the world\u2019s saviors during a pandemic without being perceived as gloating or, more important, gouging a global population in need? That\u2019s the tricky needle Pfizer has had to thread, given the company\u2019s status as one of the most effective coronavirus vaccine manufacturers in the world. Pfizer recorded 2020 revenues of about $42 billion\u2014but that largely excludes the post-winter period, when Pfizer\u2019s COVID vaccine was granted emergency use authorization by the FDA. The company expects to bring in $26 billion in COVID vaccine sales alone this year, about 60% of its total 2020 revenues, thanks to its sprawling relationships with governments and pandemic-focused organizations across the globe. An added benefit? Via its partnership with Germany\u2019s BioNTech, the maker of the \u201clittle blue pill\u201d is now synonymous with mRNA vaccine technology that could feed its pipeline in infectious disease treatments and other therapeutics for years to come.</span>"}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 77, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;How do you balance being one of the world\u2019s saviors during a pandemic without being perceived as gloating or, more important, gouging a global population in need? That\u2019s the tricky needle Pfizer has had to thread, given the company\u2019s status as one of the most effective coronavirus vaccine manufacturers in the world. Pfizer recorded 2020 revenues of about $42 billion\u2014but that largely excludes the post-winter period, when Pfizer\u2019s COVID vaccine was granted emergency use authorization by the FDA. The company expects to bring in $26 billion in COVID vaccine sales alone this year, about 60% of its total 2020 revenues, thanks to its sprawling relationships with governments and pandemic-focused organizations across the globe. An added benefit? Via its partnership with Germany\u2019s BioNTech, the maker of the \u201clittle blue pill\u201d is now synonymous with mRNA vaccine technology that could feed its pipeline in infectious disease treatments and other therapeutics for years to come.&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:12673,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\">How do you balance being one of the world\u2019s saviors during a pandemic without being perceived as gloating or, more important, gouging a global population in need? That\u2019s the tricky needle Pfizer has had to thread, given the company\u2019s status as one of the most effective coronavirus vaccine manufacturers in the world. Pfizer recorded 2020 revenues of about $42 billion\u2014but that largely excludes the post-winter period, when Pfizer\u2019s COVID vaccine was granted emergency use authorization by the FDA. The company expects to bring in $26 billion in COVID vaccine sales alone this year, about 60% of its total 2020 revenues, thanks to its sprawling relationships with governments and pandemic-focused organizations across the globe. An added benefit? Via its partnership with Germany\u2019s BioNTech, the maker of the \u201clittle blue pill\u201d is now synonymous with mRNA vaccine technology that could feed its pipeline in infectious disease treatments and other therapeutics for years to come.</span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2020, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 215, "itemDescription": "Pfizer slips 17 spots this year, in large part because of the expiration last year of one of its more lucrative patents, for the fibromyalgia drug Lyrica. That eroded the drugmaker's revenue, which fell 4% overall in 2019. But Pfizer also made some big moves: After years of rumors that it would split up, Pfizer finally slimmed down, announcing over the summer that it would merge Upjohn, its off-patent drug division, with general drug company Mylan. Meanwhile, the pharmaceutical giant found some significant efficiencies in its operations: profits surged some 46%. The company, which is developing a promising COVID-19 vaccine in partnership with German drug company BioNTech, in July announced the U.S. government had ordered $1.95 billion worth of doses of the new treatment\u2014to provide it to Americans for free, if the FDA approves the vaccine."}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 64, "itemDescription": "Pfizer slipped a few spots, in large part because of the expiration last year of one of its more lucrative patents, for the fibromyalgia drug Lyrica. That eroded the drugmaker's revenue, which fell 4% overall in 2019. But Pfizer also made some big moves: After years of rumors that it would split up, Pfizer finally slimmed down, announcing over the summer that it would merge Upjohn, its off-patent drug division, with general drug company Mylan. Meanwhile, the pharmaceutical giant found some significant efficiencies in its operations: profits surged some 46%."}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2019, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2019, "rank": 198, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2019, "rank": 61, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2018, "rank": 187, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2018, "rank": 57, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2017, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2017, "rank": 173, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2017, "rank": 54, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2016, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2016, "rank": 186, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2016, "rank": 55, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2015, "rank": 211, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year":